			{
				"type": "Comment",
				"start": 0,
				"end": 689,
				"data": "\n\tThe sibling-newline flow rule counts prose per RUN, and a run ends at whatever owns its own\n\tline: a comment, a block element, a control-flow block, a `<br />`, and an authored blank\n\tline — whether that blank stands in a whitespace-only node between two siblings or at the\n\tedge of a content text. Every case puts a two-word run on one side of the boundary and a\n\tone-word run on the other: the prose flows right up to the boundary (bounding is not\n\tsterilizing), and the label beyond it holds, where the same nodes with no boundary between\n\tthem are one run and the label flows with the prose. Prettier holds every authored newline\n\there; the flowing halves are the divergence.\n"
			},
			{
				"type": "Text",
				"start": 689,
				"end": 691,
				"raw": "\n\n",
				"data": "\n\n"
			},
			{
				"type": "Comment",
				"start": 691,
				"end": 797,
				"data": " a comment bounds the run: the prose run before it flows up to it, the one-word run after it holds "
